## The short version

Two live tests in one session. **The progress demo:** walk your vertical slice end to end for the room, claims first, and take questions. **The repo audit:** a classmate who has never seen your project clones it cold and tries to build, run, orient, and locate where a new feature would go — scoring the experience on the audit sheet. Together: is it **working**, and is it **joinable**?

---

## Why this assignment exists

This course argues that a founded project must be two things at once, and CP10 measures both with the only instruments that don't lie. A **live demo** is the honest test of "working" — slides can claim anything, but a slice walked in real time proves the need is served, the scary tech runs, and a user can get through. And a **cold audit by a stranger** is the honest test of "joinable" — your setup guide, your README, your issue tracker, your commit history, all read by someone with no context and no one to ask, which is exactly how every future contributor will meet your project.

The deeper lessons are about honest evidence. Demo craft — claims stated up front, edges admitted, a fallback rehearsed — is how professionals show work-in-progress without overselling it, a skill you'll use in every sprint review and stakeholder meeting of your career. And *a stranger working through your documentation with no one to ask* is one of the most instructive tests in software: what they trip on is never what you expect, and every stumble they write down is a gift — a precise, free specification of what to fix in the two weeks before launch. That's also why you audit someone else's repo: reading an unfamiliar codebase methodically (and grading it kindly and honestly) is the *Curious* strand made practical, and it sharpens exactly the eye you need on your own work.

